I still don't follow what the problem with using djgpp is. It hasn't
been updated because no software requires anything more - very little
code (if any aside from video) is written uses more than what a P1 can
do with minor differences between chips. The biggest change was from the
P1 to the P pro which can be multiprocessed. Windows XP runs with dos
executables. *anything* on any MS platform that isn't a com or bat file
*is* a dos executable. You can play around with an unsupported compiler
and someone not on the development team that's trying to get it to work,
but if you want something that works on every ms platform since dos 5
including 2000 and XP, use the djgpp compiler: it's derived directly
from the compiler that created linux, and it works better than the Solaris
compiler and several others. 

